# The Ornament of Oratory

Tamil title: பிரசங்க பூஷணம்

Pulavar Boologanathar looked like a king about to be crowned with the title "Ornament of Oratory." There was a reason for his delight. His lecture had been arranged for that evening in the six-pillared hall of the Alamarthadiyar temple.

"If those mischievous schoolboys see me just this evening and hear me speak, from tomorrow onward they will never again dare to misbehave, even by mistake. So many of the town's leading men will be assembled there! They will proclaim my fame, place a garland upon me, applaud in delight, and rejoice. Once those young bulls see all this, their eyes will open," thought the pulavar happily.

Everything about him, from the sandal paste on his forehead to his elaborate adornments, was a source of amusement to the schoolboys. Their teasing had become intolerable. The Ornament of Oratory therefore saw this as an opportunity to deliver a rare lecture, gain fame through it, display that fame before the pupils, and put their mischief to flight. He swelled with pride.

Arunachala Iyer, the priest of the Alamarthadiyar temple, was happy that day too. How could he earn anything from a temple inhabited only by bats? He would sit at its entrance, yawning away the day. After turning over many plans, he concluded that the villagers had to be made devoutly attached to Alamarthadiyar. Only then would many people visit the temple and produce an income.

There had been a time when he valued his hereditary right in this temple no more than a straw. He had then held a job in the city. When the planets caused trouble, he lost it. Only afterward did he become concerned with serving Alamarthadiyar.

"See that! Swami went on and on about the city, but look, Alamarthadiyar called him back. Could he ever abandon that merit?" the villagers would say.

"They lack nothing except coming anywhere near the temple! What am I to do, tied to this temple and left here swatting flies?" Arunachala Iyer would lament.

At last an idea occurred to him. He found a means of instilling devotion in people's minds. He decided to arrange a lecture that would explain the greatness of the Alamarthadiyar temple and speak more generally of the glory of the devotional path. A musical religious discourse would cost some money. He therefore invited the Ornament of Oratory, Pulavar Boologanathar, with great affection and asked him to deliver a rare lecture.

The pulavar had thirsted for just such an opportunity. As soon as Iyer made his request, the teacher gladly agreed. Before agreeing, however, he said:

"Am I really so very learned? What I have learned is a handful of earth; what I have not learned is the size of the world. When so many scholars exist, you have sought an unripe fruit while ripe fruit is at hand. Though I am a man of little intellect, with the grace of Him who bears the little moon, I stand ready to serve according to the saying of the wise, 'My duty is to labour and lie at his service.'"

Having said this, the pulavar was immensely pleased. Why would he not be? He had spoken six sentences and packed three quotations into them:

1. What has been learned is a handful of earth;
   what remains unlearned is the size of the world.
2. Seeking an unripe fruit when ripe fruit is at hand.
3. My duty is to labour and lie at his service.

Three quotations in six sentences! Was he not the Ornament of Oratory? Would he indulge in mere plain speech? What use was holding palm-leaf texts if he did not pour out the verses he had memorized? That was why half his six sentences consisted of the words of the wise.

And that was not all. He had said, "Though I am *siru-mathi udaiyen*, a man of little intelligence, I take refuge in the grace of *siru-mathi udaiyon*, the one who bears the little moon." His majestic clearing of the throat and his gaze seemed to shout: "Arunachala Iyer! What do you think of my learning? Did you see the beauty concealed in that sentence? I called myself one of little intellect, and I called Siva the one who bears the crescent moon. One phrase, two meanings! What beauty! Calling him 'the one with a little mind' even possesses the grammatical richness of praise through apparent abuse. I placed all this in a single sentence. What do you think of my skill?"

Arunachala Iyer said little. He left to prepare the man who would preside over the meeting.

The pulavar summoned one of his pupils. "Inscribe my words upon the leaves for this evening's lecture," he ordered. The pupil was himself destined to become an Ornament of Oratory, and so he sat before the pulavar with writing leaf in hand. After meditating upon the Lord, the pulavar began his address.

"Beloved friends!" he said.

The pupil wrote it down and echoed, "Beloved friends!" when he had finished.

The pulavar shook his head and cleared his throat. "No. Instead of addressing them as 'Beloved friends,' I think 'True beloved friends' would be better," he offered as a correction. "Beloved friends" was struck out and "True beloved friends" inscribed upon the leaf.

"My boy! Rather than merely saying 'True beloved friends,' I think it would be far superior to say 'True Saivite beloved friends,'" said the pulavar.

The pupil had to write his master's thoughts, and so he struck out "True beloved friends" as well and wrote "True Saivite beloved friends."

"What have you written, my boy?" the pulavar asked.

"I wrote 'True Saivite beloved friends,'" said the pupil.

The pulavar smiled. "That would not be just!"

"What would not?" asked the bearer of the writing leaf.

"Alamarthadiyar is Siva, and I too am a Saivite. Yet will only Saivites be among the friends gathered there today? How could that be? Vaishnavites will be present too. Therefore it would neither be enough nor be just to stop with 'True Saivite beloved friends.'

"It is proper to address the assembly as 'Saivite, Vaishnavite, Buddhist, Christian, and Islamic beloved friends.' Yes, write it like that," he said.

The pupil wrote it, and only then was the pulavar satisfied.

"My boy, many people do not understand this maturity. They cannot grasp even the simple fact that a great meeting may contain followers of many religions and that a speaker ought to address them all. Tamil is withering in the embrace of such people. Am I one of them? Though I am a Saivite, we must not disregard Vaishnavite, Buddhist, Christian, and Islamic friends. If we establish this virtuous practice in the country, this very earth will become Kailasa," he said.

The pupil felt like asking why, when the single phrase "Beloved friends" would have fulfilled this aim, his master had abandoned it and piled up all the rest. But could he contradict the Ornament of Oratory?

The pulavar dictated: "Is it not my great fortune that I have been asked to speak at this gathering of learned and worthy people in the Alamarthadiyar temple? If you ask whether I possess the intellectual ability to stand and speak before an assembly such as this, I shall answer that I do not. Yet you have regarded even me as someone of consequence and invited me to speak..."

The pupil wrote rapidly. Before explaining the subject any further, the pulavar had him read back the lecture thus far and listened with pleasure.

"This is also how Thiru. V. Kalyanasundaranar speaks," said the pupil. "There is just one difference. You said, 'If you ask whether I have the intellectual ability to speak before such an assembly, I shall answer that I do not.' Thiru. Vi. Ka. would not say 'No' merely once. He would say, 'No, no, no,' three times."

The pulavar laughed and told him to write.

"Some people repeat everything three times. They say, 'There is, there is, there is.' They say, 'How wonderful, how wonderful, how wonderful!' Does this threefold usage not waste time and thought? Is that good? Would it not be right to say it once? I have no such habit. I never have. I most certainly do not."

The pupil found it hard to write this. The pulavar, while declaring that threefold repetition was improper, had repeated the same thing three times. Was that proper? The pupil suffered inwardly, but how could he voice what was in his mind? The man at fault was no ordinary Tamil scholar; he was the Ornament of Oratory.

"Every public speaker torments his audience by prolonging the unnecessary section called the modest preface. That disease never approaches me. Idle words are bitter neem to me. I shall not fear to declare that the modest preface is an unnecessary, useless, tasteless, lifeless, thoughtless, disorderly, taste-profitless, profitably tasteless, conceptually disorderly, properly thoughtless, lifelessly tasteful, thoughtfully lifeless, and vitally thoughtless affair. There is nothing to fear. The saying of the wise, 'Those who recite the five letters need fear nothing,' deserves reflection here. And why should one reflect upon the sayings of the wise? Because the wise are worthy men."

Thus did the Ornament of Oratory explain beautifully the needless nature of the modest preface, while the pupil wrote.

"My boy," said the pulavar.

The pupil prepared to write.

"Do not write this. It is not for inclusion; I am telling it to you. Listen to a grammatical explanation of the public address. Forgetting the assigned subject, killing time with a useless modest preface and salutations to the assembly: that is cruel, exceedingly cruel. One must not allow the modest preface to grow too long. If you always pay close attention to my lectures, you will notice an important fact. It is the fruit of my experience and research. Namely, I do not waste time by delivering an immoderately long modest preface."

The pupil, compelled to keep his tongue under restraint, perceived that the Ornament of Oratory was wasting time by prolonging the very assertion that one must not prolong the modest preface, and that he had still not reached the subject of the lecture. Could the pupil say so? To speak would violate the discipline between guru and disciple.

Because this aside had broken the thread, the Ornament of Oratory once again asked him to read everything written thus far.

The pulavar resumed:

"'The Brahmins are those who are virtuous, for they conduct themselves with tender compassion toward every living being.' I shall call this a jewelled saying, an ornamental saying, and also a humble saying. A Brahmin possesses the path of virtue and the path of love. He possesses beautiful qualities and the capacity to show love toward every life. He alone is a Brahmin.

"Alas! One faction shamelessly tells the lie that Brahmins ruin those who trust them. The path taken by their intelligence is not the path of virtue. What crime have Brahmins committed? What harm have they done to those who trusted them? My eyes cannot perceive it.

"And when I say 'my eyes,' what eyes do I mean? If you ask whether I mean the eyes that behold the external world and run riot, I shall say no. Thrice shall I say no! Mine are the eyes that have seen not only the present time but also the kings and royal courts of the past, the learned, their moral paths, this world and the next, the Chera country, the Chola realm, the Pandya capital, the regions where many petty kings lived, the devotees, and the devotees of devotees. My eyes have beheld all this and much more.

"I beheld it in books and stood astonished! Can I refuse to trust eyes that have seen so much? It is through them that I see the Brahmins. Yet some great sinners who do not follow the path of the wise say unspeakable things against them. My heart aches. I cannot bear this cruelty even a little. Brahmins have committed no wrong; they do no wrong; they do not even know the way to do wrong. Is that not the very reason they were born Brahmins?

"Here sits my friend, Arunachala Iyer, an authority on the Vedas and sastras. Upon his face one may behold the dance of the maiden robed in white, the Goddess of Learning. In his heart rest the feet of the Lord. He knows not the least deceit. I shall say one thing: I will climb a hill and proclaim that I attained my present good position only through our Iyer's gracious compassion and assistance. I shall shout until the ears of those low people who insult the Brahmin community are ruined.

"You praised my learning. You admired my hymn. You conferred a title upon me. You read my book. And what is that book? Something I composed. How? I composed it through divine grace. How did divine grace reach me? Here is my friend and teacher of wisdom, Arunachala Iyer, who brought that grace to me. He stirred and encouraged me to write, and I wrote. You showered me with affection. Is he not the cause? He is a Brahmin. I am not of the Brahmin community, yet it was a Brahmin who gave me a helping hand. It was he who made you aware of my scholarship. Why do certain people revile such a Brahmin community? They do not reflect upon their own ignorance. Siva! Siva! Sacrilege! Sacrilege! These fallen, quarrelsome people seek disgrace in both this world and the next."

The pulavar poured all this forth in a torrent, and the pupil wrote until his hand hurt.

When that section was done, the Ornament of Oratory wiped away his sweat and said, "My boy, always listen carefully to one thing. You must invariably speak in praise of Brahmins. Do you know why? We are Tamils. Whatever we do, whatever we say, and however we behave, our Tamil people will not destroy us. Brahmins, however, are not of our race. Aryanism has entered, subdued, and corrupted our people. If we obstruct its course even now, it will cause us trouble. Therefore, if in our lectures we praise, commend, and support Brahmins, they will cause us no trouble.

"Tamils will become angry. They will ask, 'Why does this pulavar support Brahmins like this, without any attachment to his own people?' What if they do? My friend, I shall ask, 'Which Brahmin did I support? Today's hardened sinners? Good heavens! Am I a log of wood or a clay doll, that I should feel no anger? I praised *anthanars*, men who follow the path of love and grace. Are those qualities found in the Brahmin caste?' When we say that, our Tamil comrades will pat us on the back. Thus we shall acquire honour and influence among both Aryans and Dravidians."

The pupil promised to follow this virtuous practice.

The pulavar was about to resume dictating the lecture. First the pupil read it once more so that the thread would be clear. The pulavar began again with renewed vigour, then stopped in confusion.

"My boy! Did you see a green sheet of paper?"

"No. What paper?" asked the pupil.

"The invitation to today's lecture," said the Ornament of Oratory.

He ran inside and turned over his books. The handbill was nowhere to be found, and he grew distressed. He asked his wife.

"Who knows in which heap of rubbish it lies?" she replied sharply.

"To your eyes it is rubbish! Foolish woman! Rubbish, indeed! What do you know of the rubies and emeralds lying in that rubbish?" the pulavar rebuked her.

But was his wife not the very embodiment of Sakti? She did not let him off so easily.

"At least a hen scratches through rubbish and finds a worm or insect. What have you found after scratching through this rubbish again and again?" she asked.

"Fool! Foolish woman! Gold-greedy foolish woman! Why crave gold and possessions? Know that even a needle without an eye will not accompany us on the final road," said the pulavar. At that moment he was leafing through a book of Pattinathar's verses, searching for the missing notice.

"The eyeless needle has certainly not come, however much you have come here shouting and shouting," Sakti retorted.

Boologanathar had no answer. He did not even know what to say. Only the phrase "Renounce without telling anyone" entered his mind and began to torment him.

"Rubbish, she says! The jewels strung by Kamban are rubbish!" the Ornament of Oratory muttered, beating the dust from his books.

"Sir has come to see you," the pupil announced.

The pulavar hurried out. Seeing Arunachala Iyer in the hall, he said, "It is only at six this evening, is it not? Have you come already? I have not yet finished preparing my notes."

"There is no hurry. You may prepare them at leisure," said Iyer.

Only then did the Ornament of Oratory feel relieved. He had not yet decided where in the lecture to insert the *Kural*, the *Naladiyar*, the *Nanneri*, the *Tevaram*, the *Tiruvacakam*, songs of Pattinathar, and songs of Thayumanavar. He had forgotten the actual subject of the lecture. He had been searching for the wretched green handbill precisely to discover what subject had been assigned to him. It was nowhere to be seen. Would the Ornament of Oratory not be frightened when Iyer arrived at such a moment? Fortunately, Iyer said there was no hurry. The pulavar rejoiced that he could prepare the lecture splendidly.

"The chairman is ready, is he not?" he asked Iyer.

"A fine question! He is positively trembling with eagerness," said Iyer.

"Ah! Our zamindar has immense knowledge of and affection for Tamil. He possesses a poetic heart," said the Ornament of Oratory, praising the Alur zamindar who was to preside that day. Even as he spoke, a touch of fear appeared on his face.

"Ah! What have I done? Have I not acted like a fool? At the very beginning of the lecture I ought to praise the zamindar's distinction, the glory of his lineage, the honour of his family, his generosity, and his majestic bearing. Should I not melt his heart by comparing him to the three crowned Tamil kings who fostered Tamil in ancient times? Instead of writing that, I have written all manner of other things. This lecture will not do. It must be rewritten. I must compose a *viruttam* praising the zamindar's character. Yes, yes! There is a great deal of work, but Iyer said there was no hurry. I can prepare it," he thought.

Iyer continued: "Our zamindar is determined to conduct this festival on a magnificent scale. I thought we could stop with a lecture. He said that would not do and insisted upon a vocal concert and a dance recital in addition to the pulavar's lecture. He is adamant that the Ornament of Oratory be presented with a great garland of roses. I told him I had prepared a jasmine garland. He became furious. 'What learning he has! What intelligence! What research! And you would insult him with a mere jasmine garland? Put that around the neck of your temple cow,' he said. I promised to buy a rose garland.

"He has arranged for the Chintamani Sisters to give the dance recital and Palaniappan to give the vocal concert. He will bear the expense. A fine party has been arranged for the *nayanam* too. I had imagined conducting a simple lecture, but it has become a grand festival.

"Do you know one more special feature? Chintamani, who is to perform the dance, is going to become a *dasi* of our Alamarthadiyar temple. The ceremony of branding her with the temple seal will also take place. To tell you the truth, the reason the zamindar has transformed the arrangements into something so splendid is his intimacy with Chintamani. He has even asked us to change the subject of your lecture. You are to speak on 'The Art of Dance and the Progress of the Nation,'" Arunachala Iyer announced happily.

The pulavar was delighted to hear that the function had become so grand. Yet he was troubled about what he could say concerning dance. The confusion vanished at once. Bah! What was so difficult? The *Silappathikaram* existed, did it not? Taking courage, he said:

"It is our good fortune that the zamindar has taken such an interest. I shall speak about the art of dance just as he wishes. What obstacle can there be? It is one of the fine arts. The Lord reveals it at Tillai. What it creates is bliss itself. The universe is a stage! We are all the novelties of the dance. He makes us dance."

"Excellent! Excellent! Chintamani will be enchanted when she hears this lecture," said Iyer.

"Tamil possesses the substance called sweetness," the pulavar said proudly.

"Once Chintamani becomes a *dasi* of our temple, enormous crowds will come. You can deliver a lecture every week," Iyer said with delight.

The words tasted a little bitter to the pulavar, but he endured them.

"Very well. I shall come to the temple at six," said the pulavar, thinking that only when this fellow left quickly could he search out the *Silappathikaram*.

"I told you of the changes the zamindar made to the arrangements, but I forgot to mention the most important change. He has changed the date of the lecture. The function will be next Friday," said Iyer.

The Ornament of Oratory was struck speechless with grief. How much trouble since morning! How much thought and preparation! And at the end they had so casually told him that the lecture was not today. Iyer took his leave. The pulavar sent the writing pupil home and repeatedly read and savoured the rare lecture he had prepared thus far. What else could the poor man do?

The pupil came running back at that very moment.

"I had completely forgotten! You forgot too! We have already arranged for you to give a lecture at the Punnaivananathar temple next Friday. How can both be held on the same day?" he asked.

"My boy, is that such a marvel? Can one not conduct two lectures on the same day? Is the title Ornament of Oratory meaningless? Foolish child! The Punnaivananathar temple is only three miles from here. I will speak there at three in the afternoon, while this meeting will begin only after six. If I send word, the zamindar will dispatch his carriage," said the pulavar.

"Very well. I came merely to remind you in any case," said the pupil.

"It is good that you reminded me. I had indeed forgotten. Yes, I had no recollection. What subject have they arranged for me to speak about at the Punnaivananathar temple?" the pulavar asked.

With some fear, the pupil replied, "The Evils of the Art of Dance."

As soon as those words entered the ears of the Ornament of Oratory, it seemed to him that the entire universe was dancing. What could he do?

"All right! Get out!" he cried, directing his anger at the pupil. Where else could he direct it?

Another song entered his mind and began to torment him:

> You were born on a Friday,
> Raja Desingu;
> your horse was born on a Friday,
> Raja Desingu;
> the battle was born on a Friday,
> Raja Desingu.

4 February 1945

---NOTES---
The first two pages had no OCR text and were transcribed directly from the scans before translation. The title `பிரசங்க பூஷணம்` is intentionally grand and ironic; "The Ornament of Oratory" preserves both senses. Repetitions, stacked synonyms, quotations, and self-contradictions have been retained because they form the story's central satire. Period institutional terms such as *dasi*, *nayanam*, *viruttam*, and the titles of Tamil classics are transliterated where a compact English equivalent would erase their historical setting. Apparent OCR damage and uncertain readings are documented separately.
